Assistant: I see the issue. The problem is that we're trying to render an async component directly in the preview. We need to modify the preview component to handle the async nature of the imported component. Here's the fix:

<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/SamlIdpPage-5a6274b8.tsx">
<change>
<description>Convert the preview component to handle async component properly</description>
<content>
import React, { Suspense } from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';
import ImportedComponent from '../../app/(use-page-wrapper)/auth/saml-idp/page';

function AsyncComponentWrapper({ params, searchParams }) {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
      <ImportedComponent params={params} searchParams={searchParams} />
    </Suspense>
  );
}

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({
    params: {
      type: "string",
      value: "{}",
      label: "Params",
    },
    searchParams: {
      type: "string",
      value: '{"code": "example_code"}',
      label: "Search Params",
    },
  });

  const params = JSON.parse(state.params.value);
  const searchParams = JSON.parse(state.searchParams.value);

  return <AsyncComponentWrapper params={params} searchParams={searchParams} />;
}
</content>
</change>
</file>

The changes made:

1. Added `Suspense` import from React
2. Created an `AsyncComponentWrapper` component that wraps the imported component with Suspense
3. Added a loading fallback for better UX
4. Wrapped the imported component with Suspense to properly handle the async nature of the component

This should resolve the Promise object rendering error by properly handling the async component with Suspense. The Suspense component will show a loading state while the async component is being resolved.

